[COMMENTS]
STATION,Crump profile weir built in 1968 to replace previous rated section. In the winter of 2022/23 the existing Crump Weir was converted into a compound Crump weir with the installation of a section of lower weir crest and a divide wall. Compensation flow from Clatworthy and smaller Luxhay Reservoirs in the headwaters maintain low flows. GENERAL: Located upstream of Taunton, the station is important for flood warning. Opened in 1961 as a rated section with cableway and with an unstable bed, 50 m upstream of a road bridge. Crump profile weir built in 1968 (breadth 12.2m). In the winter of 2022/23, the existing Crump weir was converted into a compound Crump weir with the installation of a section of lower weir crest and a divide wall. STATION HYDROMETRY: Full range station. Out-of-bank flow first occurs below bankfull; during extreme events weir is drowned by backwater from downstream bridge, causing floodplain inundation. The original single crest weir installed in 1968 was converted into a 2 crested compound Crump weir in the winter of 2022/23. This was to improve the accuracy and sensitivity of the structure at low flows and to aid fish passage. The limbs for the structure are derived theoretically from the physical dimensions. The in-bank rating beyond the modular limit of the structure is derived by current meter gaugings. The top section for above bankfull flows was derived using a hydraulic model. Small gap in rating history between February and March 2023 during the construction phase, where temporary ratings were in place, however no Peak Flows affected during this period. FLOW RECORD: As a velocity-area station up to 03/1968 flows were unreliable below 1.42 cumecs. Full period of record peak flow data reviewed and released in September 2020 (WINFAP Files v9). FLOW REGIME: Minor surface water abstractions for public water supply; also abstraction for agriculture. Compensation flow from Clatworthy and smaller Luxhay Reservoirs in the headwaters maintain low flows but neither reservoir is large enough to influence the catchment's fairly rapid response to rainfall.
CATCHMENT,Fairly responsive catchment draining from Brendon Hills, predominantly comprises sandstones in the upper- and clays in the lower-catchment. Land use is rural. No major catchment changes.
Qmed Suitability,Gauged above QMED.
Pooling Suitability,Gauged to within 3% of AMAX3. Considers non modular flow and fits well to gaugings.
[END]
